Date: Mon, 10 May 93 23:45:45 EDT
From: carlf@ai.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: Nanotech Beanstalks?

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

     Thanks for the response, Carl. Just one more question, has anyone
   ever calculated the tensile strength of neutronium? I'd be interested
   in the ratio of its strength to diamond fiber.

Its strength is known both theoretically and observationally (from
observing earthquakes on neutron stars).  I don't know what it is, but
I would guess it is about 10^8 times stronger than diamond, since this
is roughly the ratio of the strength of the electromagnetic and strong
forces.

     How come neutron stars don't beta-decay, or do they?

That's two questions!  But I'll answer it anyway.

It's similar to the reason stable nuclei don't beta-decay.  A neutron
only decays if the energy of the decay products is less than that of
the neutron.  The decay products are a proton, an electron, and an
antineutrino.  In a neutron star, the electrons are very compressed,
so creating a new one requires squeezing it into a high-pressure crowd
of existing electrons.  This consumes more energy than would be
liberated by the neutron decay.

--carlf

