Date: Tue, 11 May 93 12:56:44 +0100
From: Rich Walker <grlw1@cus.cam.ac.uk>
Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech 

Perry criticises the use of asynchronous circuits. I respond.

Metastability only occurs when you need to synchronise to the synchronous
world.  Simple solution: eliminate it. It's actually fairly easy to do, as
RAM can easily be made async., busses often are, and so forth.

Timing problems are even more easily solved: use C (Muller) elements.  (For
k inputs, when they agree, set the output to the value of the inputs.
Otherwise, leave the output as it was). Then, use a more interesting
variation on 2-level logic, and arrive fairly rapidly at a working async. 
circuit.

True, if you design it in a conventional means, it's sweat and blood.
However, it _is_ possible to do it, provided you take this slightly
different approach.  The only real problem is the lack of 74 series TTL with
REQUEST/ACKNOWLEDGE wires on it...

