Date: Tue, 11 May 93 06:38:02 PDT
From: GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov
Subject: Neutron Stars (was Nanotech Beanstalks)

Ray Cromwell asks:

>How come neutron stars don't beta-decay, or do they? 

The short answer is that the presence of degenerate electrons
in the star effectively blocks the decay process, and stabilizes
the nucleus.

Degenerate matter can stabilize particles that would normally
decay under laboratory conditions. The process of neutron
enrichment that enables this to occur is called neutronization.

Reference: Astrophysics I by Bowers and Deeming, p. 288.

