Date: Tue, 11 May 93 9:14:49 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Synaptic Storage Requirements

Another note on the storage requirements for simulating synapses:

  Jacob T. Schwartz, The New Connectionism:  Developing Relationships
  Between Neuroscience and Artificial Intelligence.  Daedalus 117:1.
  Winter 1988. Pp 123-142.

In this article, Schwartz is interested in estimating the computational
power of the brain; when considering the requirements for representing
the states of synapses, he guesses that eight bits for long-term
strength "may well suffice".  However, he makes an interesting point
that the instantaneous state of a synapse (which modulates its function,
as does the long-term "strength") must also be stored.  These
shorter-duration synaptic modifications "may last for as little as
fifty milliseconds or for as long as several seconds, minutes, or
days".  A synapse is a complex machine; Schwartz estimates 32 bits
for shorter-term synaptic state, for a total of 40 bits of storage
per synapse.  Schwartz uses a figure of 1,000 trillion as the number
of synapses in the brain; I think that 100 trillion is probably more
accurate (see Cherniak's "The Bounded Brain"*, which Ralph Merkle
referenced a few months ago on this list -- oops, actually that's
_eight_ months ago, so here's a quote of the reference):

> "The Bounded Brain: Toward Quantitative Neuroanatomy"
> by Christopher Cherniak
> Journal of Cognitive Neuroscience
> Volume 2, Number 1
> pages 58-68

Anyway, that then works out to 4,000 trillion bits, or about
500 terabytes (though Schwartz's estimate was considerably higher
because he decided that both sides of a synaptic junction, as
well as the gap, required lots of state).  This is my (perhaps
optimistic) guess.

derek
* Note:  though Cherniak's article is wonderful regarding structural
properties and counts of neurons, synapses, and the area of cortical
sheet, his estimate of storage per synapse -- 1 bit -- is made in
an offhand way without any analysis.  Disappointing in an otherwise
really cool paper.

