From: Brian.Hawthorne@East.Sun.COM (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: CRYONICS: Re: Reviving a neuro suspendee

> I agree that to attempt to get it perfect would be torture, but I don't
> believe that we need to do that.  I think that "close is good enough".
> The mind will learn and adapt to it's new inputs.
> 
> In support of this belief I'd site the experiment in which a subject
> would wear a prism over his eyes which would flip his vision up-side-down.
> After a day or so, he adjusts quite well to the new view.  The wierd
> thing is that after removing the device, he again sees everything
> up-side-down for a while and needs to get used to his original eyes again.
> 

Or the current (admittedly crude) attempts at vision prostheses. You take
a very small video camera, do some signal processing to get basic outlines
and the like, and "display" the results on a small array (on the order 
of 100x100) of pins, by moving the pins in and out. The display
board is strapped onto your forehead (don't worry, the pins are not sharp).

After several days, people who were totally blind before, are able to 
"see" in a rudimentary, but useful, fashion. The brain rapidly adapts to
this new input.

This is why the nerve matching problem is not impossible. Currently, 
severed hands and feet can be reattached, and some of the function and
sensation can be restored. 

Indeed, I hypothesize that direct brain-CPU links will be possible
once we better understand neurons. We may have no idea of what kind
of information the neurons encode, but if we know the physical mechanisms,
we could just provide an appropriate interface. Software? Let the brain
do that. Granted, when you first hooked up, you would probably have 
horribly confusing sensations, but as long as the inputs were ordered
in SOME way, I predict your brain would straighten them out into
something meaningful.

