>the contract existed. For $5 ExI will keep a database entry on the
>contracted relationship and base it's action on that.)

Whoa.  Many people, including Harry, have expressed a strong
preference for simplicity.  So let's seperate the issue of charging
for judgements are related effort from the issue of allowing list PPL.
I'd hate for folks to reject PPL on the grounds that "all that
charging is too complicated".

The choice here is between all disputes going to the list
administrator (LA), vs. some of them being judged privately.  Either
judgements might have to be enforced by the LA.  But unless private
judges issue verdicts that tend to cost more to enforce, the PPL issue
is independent of the charging issue.  If contract registration with
ExI were required for PPL, but not for LA judgements, then that might
be a basis for distinction.  But I see no reason why the private
judges, or individual people can't hold documentation of their
contracts, and just submit said documentation when requesting a
verdict be enforced.  Or if registries are needed, I would be happy to
offer registration services at less than $5 a contract!

Regarding costs and charging, it would seem that if the LA is going to
charge to implement private verdicts, he/she should also charge to
implement LA list verdicts.  And if implementation is charged for, why
not have the LA also charge for doing their judging.  This would
especially encourage a PPL of possibly cheaper judges.  But charging
only for PPL judging/enforcement, but not for list
judging/enforcement, is an effective recipe for killing off PPL.

Robin Hanson

