Subject: Nanotech Beanstalks?

Re: Mr. Cromwell's comment on diamond fiber.  

You source is plain wrong, or doesn't understand how to design
engineering structures.  Time for beanstalk design 101.  The
minimum weight structure on which you can mount an elevator
and ride to orbit is a compressive tower meetin a hanging
tether.  The tower would be tapered as you go up (think
of the Eiffel Tower), and the cable would be narrowest
at the bottom, and get thicker as you approach GEO.

Good quality diamonds have been used as anvils to squish
minerals to reproduce pressures deep inside the Earth.
Pressures of 1 million atmospheres are regularly produced.
So let us take the compressive strength of diamond as
15 million psi.  The density of diamond is 3.5 g/cc, so a
column of diamond of constant section 3000 km tall can
just support it's own weight under 1 gravity.

If we taper the cross sectional area by a factor of 10 from
bottom to top, we can go twice this, which puts us at the
dept of the Earth's gravity well (6378 km).  

I don't have data on the tensile strength of diamond, but
it seems clear from the above that it is perfectly possible
to build a diamond tower up to orbit, given a way to mass
produce diamond structural members.

Dani Eder

