Frankly, I find such numerology of little use.  Drake's equation estimated that
there are on average around 1.5 intelligent civilizations in the Milky Way
galaxy at any given time.  This number was the result of a probabilistic
argument including such constants as `probability of appropriately sized
planets within a life belt around a star', `probability of appropriate
compounds existing in appropriate amounts', `avg. lifetime of a star/planet',
etc.  In some of these the value was estimated to within several orders of
magnitude!  Any thoughts as to the validity of the final result?  IMHO
Schwartz's bit estimates are equally useless...

Computational speed is another issue of current speculation.  Carl Feynman
writes:

>...Well, the amount of information a human can
>learn into long-term memory is limited to about 1 bit/second.  So
>someone like me, who is about 10^9 seconds old, can't know more than
>125 megabytes consciously.   Add a factor of 10 for unconsciously
>known information (skills and expectations) and the total is only 1.25
>Gbytes.  So the total memory capacity needed is well within the
>abiliities of large modern computers.

First of all, where does this value of learning 1 bit/second come from.  We
learn one bit in every synapse?  One bit in all the synapses per second?
Are we talking serial processing or parallel?  How parallel?
A factor of ten for "unconsciously known information"?  Please clarify how this
value was deduced...

Sorry to be so critical, but I find such speculative estimates both
unrealistic and completely uninformative.  Even if some reasonable estimate
of brain capacity in bits were to be calculated, what would it tell us?

A related question:  "How big of a balloon is needed to go to the Moon?"

Paul


